WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.490
REV. ADAM RICK: Almighty
Father, creator and sustainer

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.630
of us all, you have brought
us safely to this day.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.917
Give us minds to
thank you for all

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:20.125
of your providential mercies.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.210
Lord Jesus Christ, only son
of the Father and Savior

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.300
of the world, you have shed upon
us the light of your intoning

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.160
embrace.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.310
Give us hearts to
love you in return.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.970
Holy Spirit, advocate
and guide, you

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.020
have made us to be fit
vessels for your glory.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.120
Give us wills to
walk in step with you

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.470
to bring forth your fruits.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.180
Most glorious and
blessed Trinity,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.050
ever one God, by the blood
of the eternal covenant

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.390
you have adopted us as children
into your divine family.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.270
We ask now that you
so guide our minds,

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:04.459
so fill our imaginations,
so control our wills

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:06.860
that we may be wholly yours--

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.450
utterly dedicated to you.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.750
And then use us, we pray,
as you will and always

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.590
to your glory and the
welfare of your people.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.660
This we ask with boldness
on the merits of Jesus

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.410
Christ our Savior.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:19.910
Amen.

00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:23.540
Please turn and face
the flag with me.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.027
ALL: I pledge allegiance
to the flag of the United

00:01:30.027 --> 00:01:35.120
States of America, and to the
republic for which it stands,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.310
one nation under God,
indivisible, with liberty

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.268
and justice for all.

00:01:41.268 --> 00:01:43.763
[ORGAN PLAYING]

00:01:53.244 --> 00:01:55.739
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:02:55.346 --> 00:02:58.334
[ORGAN PLAYING, CHOIR SINGING]

00:03:57.596 --> 00:04:00.086
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:04.184
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:08:10.978 --> 00:08:18.962
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:10:10.738 --> 00:10:13.233
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:12:13.191 --> 00:12:15.676
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:12:39.035 --> 00:12:41.520
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.400
SPEAKER: Good morning to
all and welcome to the fall

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:07.870
convocation.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:11.130
A special thanks to our
organist, Dr. Derek Stauff,

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.120
to the Hillsdale
College Chamber Choir

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.320
under the direction of Professor
James Holleman and Debbi Wyse,

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:21.310
and to Reverend Adam Rick for
the invocation and benediction.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:23.440
Distinguished members
of the platform party

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.300
today are Dr. Larry
Arnn, president.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:28.780
Dr. Michael Ward, our speaker.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:31.790
Dr. Mark Kalthoff,
dean of faculty.

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:35.270
Dr. Matthew Young, dean
of natural sciences.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:38.330
Dr. Stephen Smith,
dean of humanities.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:41.810
Dr. Paul Moreno, dean
of the social sciences.

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:44.425
Dr. RJ Pestritto, graduate dean.

00:13:44.425 --> 00:13:48.030
And Reverend Adam
Rick, college chaplain.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:51.970
Now for the scholarships.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:52.970
Hope this the right one.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:54.142
Nope, wrong one.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:00.270
All right, the all-school
grade point average

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:04.860
for Spring 2019, 3.399.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:06.400
This is the same every time.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.490
The all women's average--

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:14.170
so remember, 3.399-- all
women's average, 3.486.

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:17.556
The all men's average, 3.317.

00:14:17.556 --> 00:14:22.590
With a GPA of 3.520, the
sorority scholarship goes to Pi

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:23.418
Beta Phi.

00:14:23.418 --> 00:14:25.210
Would a representative
please come forward.

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:27.700
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:59.270
With a GPA of 3.302, the
fraternity scholarship

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.240
goes to Alpha Tau Omega.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:03.324
Would a representative
please come forward.

00:15:03.324 --> 00:15:05.809
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.690
Larry P. Arnn is the 12th
president of Hillsdale College.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:37.410
He received his BA from Arkansas
State University, graduated

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:39.270
with highest distinction.

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.820
He received his MA and
his PhD in government

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:44.010
from the Claremont
Graduate School.

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.630
He also studied
in England, first,

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:47.790
as a research student
in international history

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.770
at the London
School of Economics

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.740
and then in modern history
at Oxford University.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.070
While in England he also
served as director for research

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:59.110
for the Sir Martin-- for
the late Sir Martin Gilbert,

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:01.080
the official biographer
of Winston Churchill.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.540
He returned to the United
States to become editor

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:05.790
for Public Research,
Syndicated, and he

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:08.220
served as president of
the Claremont Institute,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.500
an educational
research institution

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.390
based in Southern California.

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:14.850
While at Claremont, he
was the founding chairman

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.250
of the California Civil
Rights Initiative,

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:20.910
which was passed by
California voters in 1996,

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:24.210
and prohibited racial preference
in state hiring, contracting,

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:25.830
and admissions.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:27.810
Dr. Arnn is on the
board of directors

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:31.050
at the Heritage Foundation,
the Henry Salvatore Center

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:34.170
for Claremont McKenna College,
the Intercollegiate Studies

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.870
Institute, and the
Claremont Institute.

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:40.290
He served on the US Army War
College Board of Visitors

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.000
for two years, for
which he earned

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.970
the Department of Army's
Outstanding Civilian Service

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:45.810
Medal.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:49.410
In 2015, Dr. Arnn
received the Bradley Prize

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:52.260
from the Lynde Harry
Bradley Foundation.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:55.280
He is a member of the American
Political Science Association,

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:58.350
the International Churchill
Society, the Mont Pelerin

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:01.980
Society, the Philadelphia
Society, and the Philanthropy

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.090
Roundtable.

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:06.010
Published widely in national
newspapers, magazines,

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:09.569
and periodicals on issues
of public policy, history,

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:13.109
and political theory, he is
the author of three books--

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:14.819
Liberty and Learning
The Evolution

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:18.530
of American Education, The
Founders' Key The Divine

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:20.819
and Natural Connection
Between the Declaration

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:24.210
and the Constitution and
What We Risk By Losing It,

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:27.690
and Churchill's Trial Winston
Churchill and The Salvation

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.700
of Free Government.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.640
Dr. Arnn and his wife,
Penelope, have four children--

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.040
Katie, Henry, Alice, and Tony.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.554
Please join me in
welcoming Dr. Larry Arnn

00:17:37.554 --> 00:17:39.974
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:51.303
LARRY ARNN: Good morning.

00:17:51.303 --> 00:17:52.470
Can you hear in the balcony?

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:56.130
Ah, it works now.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.690
Welcome.

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:03.750
This building was built
with this service in mind.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.400
We hope it works for it.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:08.670
I see the Academy has
showed up in force,

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:13.920
and their behavior was
OK, I thought, coming in.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.490
Good job.

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:17.687
Is Duncan Stroik here?

00:18:17.687 --> 00:18:18.520
Supposed to be here.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:19.437
I was looking for him.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:22.950
Oh, he's up there.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.528
What are you doing up there?

00:18:24.528 --> 00:18:25.820
AUDIENCE: So I can hear better.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:28.160
LARRY ARNN: So he
can hear, yeah.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:30.750
There was a problem.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.730
Duncan Stroik designed
this building,

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.431
and we should
thank him for that.

00:18:34.431 --> 00:18:36.886
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:52.060
I see that several senior people
in the college administration

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:56.170
are sitting on the dangerous,
precarious front row there.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:58.750
And if one of them falls off,
there'll be a job opening.

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:02.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:10.440
Are you curious who actually
built this building?

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:12.120
They sit right there.

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:13.770
Would you stand up, please?

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:20.326
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:13.070
This building is produced by
the love of millions of people.

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:16.510
And that includes those people--

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:18.610
I visited this building
many more times

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:21.233
than I had time to do
and many more times

00:20:21.233 --> 00:20:22.900
than I've ever built
in another building

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:26.860
because it's wondrous
to watch it unfold.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:29.190
And I know-- is
Larry Weigand here?

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:30.625
No, he couldn't come.

00:20:30.625 --> 00:20:31.500
Tell him I said this.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.690
I said it before.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:36.660
Weigand Construction Company was
the general contractor on this,

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:40.442
and about halfway through
they told Mr. [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:40.442 --> 00:20:42.900
that they were going to lose
about a million bucks on this.

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.160
I think it was more than that.

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:45.660
Why?

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.820
Because nobody's built
anything like this forever.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:53.190
So it's hard to estimate what it
would cost, what it would take.

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.690
And then they told
us a great thing.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:56.232
They told us not--
they weren't going

00:20:56.232 --> 00:20:59.460
to tell these people that,
because they were trying

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:01.440
to build this building perfect.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:04.750
And they didn't want
them to stop doing that.

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:07.230
So the names of
all of these people

00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:09.060
are going to go
on a plaque that's

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:13.200
going to be put up in here
and preserved to thank them

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:14.200
for their service.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.990
It's spectacular to watch.

00:21:16.990 --> 00:21:21.020
Thank you for showing
me what you did.

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:27.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.710
We held this service
for something north

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:38.250
of 160 years in the
College Baptist Church

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:39.210
that's down the street.

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:42.720
It was built in a partnership
between the college

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:44.460
and the town--

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:47.730
started in the 1850s.

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:50.350
Over the years things changed.

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:57.090
The college never owned that
church, but it was-- it's home.

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.470
And over the years
things changed.

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:01.380
The college has a
glorious history,

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:07.350
but like all long histories,
it has its modulations.

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:10.830
And they altered
the structure so

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:12.660
that it would seat
fewer people, and they

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:16.740
had more space for other kinds
of activities in the back.

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.540
And the college grew.

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:24.510
And so we thought, we
have to do something.

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:27.720
About 20 years ago, we
made a plan for the campus.

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:33.470
And I was the one who said
we have to have a chapel.

00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:35.610
I didn't know what I was saying.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.980
I knew enough to know that
it would be difficult,

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:42.020
cost a lot of money,
not yield much revenue--

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:43.160
that kind of thing.

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:46.600
There's so many things
around here like that.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:49.550
And I didn't know
where it should be.

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:54.390
And back then, it could not have
been where it sits right here.

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.530
I noticed that the
chapel places me

00:22:56.530 --> 00:23:01.930
in a reverie when I come in
here because one forgets--

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.260
life is so busy.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:06.510
One forgets the things
that have happened.

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:08.670
And here, this is a
reminder of things

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:12.070
that had to happen for
this chapel to be here.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:15.680
And when we began, we didn't
know what those things were.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:17.190
This back of the campus--

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:21.440
we had to remove an obstacle--

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:24.320
our largest classroom building.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:28.160
And it has been described
by great architects as ugly.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:34.670
And these guys over here,
they had their offices in it,

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:38.096
and they were so
happy to be in there.

00:23:38.096 --> 00:23:44.090
And there was a large crowd
of faculty gathered when

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:47.910
we started tearing it down.

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:50.670
There was a great chomper,
and it-- it's a big old brick

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:53.190
building-- brick
inside and out, as was

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:54.810
the style here for a while.

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:58.275
And the chomper bit into it,
and there were loud cheers.

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:02.570
And then I ran up
to the foreman.

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:05.365
I said, wait, that's
the wrong building.

00:24:05.365 --> 00:24:07.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:16.920
The student union is here
now, and this fits with that.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:18.813
Duncan has made it so.

00:24:18.813 --> 00:24:20.980
Eventually, something is
going to happen over there.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:24.810
I don't know what and in God's
good time, but what's over

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:27.610
there is going to fit with this.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:30.750
And the center of the
campus is an integrity now.

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:34.050
We're going to redo the back
of Central Hall, God giving us

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:35.460
the dough.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:37.710
And it's going to look like
the front of Central Hall.

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:40.690
And it's going to match
with this back here.

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:42.620
And David Whalen
will have a quarrel

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:44.510
about the window
with Duncan Stroik

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:46.100
and whether it should
match the window

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:49.080
in the front of the Central
Hall or whether is should match

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.650
that window right up there.

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:53.910
So far the betting is
Duncan is going to win.

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.590
He won the last one.

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:58.430
And David sallied
forth many times.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:04.440
So you see, this
is a development.

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:05.820
Development toward what?

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:14.080
The college has been trying
to become fully itself

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:16.820
since the beginning--

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:19.300
intensely in recent years.

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:20.420
Why?

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:25.250
Everywhere around
us, there's a crisis.

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:26.800
There's decay.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:31.070
There's simmering conflict or
actual conflict everywhere,

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:35.150
and the conflict concerns
every fundamental thing.

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:36.890
What does family mean?

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:39.540
What does God mean?

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:41.492
Do people who love God--

00:25:41.492 --> 00:25:44.570
are they committing some injury
against their neighbors--

00:25:44.570 --> 00:25:49.390
which is a public
claim often made now?

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:57.740
And so in a time like
that, CS Lewis writes,

00:25:57.740 --> 00:26:00.770
things come to a
point, and there's

00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:05.200
not as much neutral ground
as there used to be.

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:09.500
And so we are
responding to that.

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:15.610
And we're all here today to
thank God that this happened.

00:26:15.610 --> 00:26:20.440
Because we who were actually
in charge of making it happen,

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:25.140
we didn't know until the
last step what it would take.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:29.310
Most plans are made
through principle and hope.

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:34.775
And there's an element of
practical judgment required,

00:26:34.775 --> 00:26:36.400
but when you look at
a thing like this,

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:40.350
you see that that's
not such a big element.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:45.230
So much has to happen that
you can't just make happen.

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:48.360
We should remember to
be grateful for that.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:51.850
Your students are going to carry
the college into the future.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:56.670
That's actually your job, to
learn what it has to teach.

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:58.770
Understand that you've
come to it in a time that's

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:00.270
very different
here than anywhere

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:04.350
else, because the things
that we were made to teach

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.360
are what we seek to teach.

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:11.560
And not many people learn that.

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:15.030
You are the point.

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:16.760
Think about this building.

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:18.830
Think about the
relationship it represents

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:25.650
between faith and learning,
between faith and freedom.

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:29.470
Think about that, and for
the rest of your life,

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:31.590
teach others about
it, because they

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:32.840
don't get the chance to learn.

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:53.340
I always make a hash of this,
so I'm going to do it again.

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:59.410
It's not the Emily
"Daw-tree" Award--

00:27:59.410 --> 00:28:01.900
it's the Emily Daugherty Award.

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:04.700
Does anybody know
if that's right?

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:07.250
And the woman who
gave this anonymously

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:09.830
is a particular friend
of mine, and her name

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:12.070
is Susan [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:12.070 --> 00:28:16.490
And I always say her name,
and I always mispronounce

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:18.480
the name of her mother.

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:19.190
It's crazy.

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:24.050
But we are grateful to her,
because she thought this up.

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:26.890
And it maybe is one of the--

00:28:26.890 --> 00:28:27.680
it's not maybe.

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:29.280
It's one of the
best awards we give.

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:29.780
Why?

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:34.310
Because it's given in the fall
to a junior faculty member--

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:37.520
and in the spring
to a senior one--

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:40.100
for being an excellent teacher.

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.840
And that is the point
around here, isn't it?

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:45.280
And there's so many of
you sitting over there.

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:49.060
If you stay here long
enough, you're going win it.

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:52.452
Or else, somebody's
going to kill you.

00:28:52.452 --> 00:28:54.910
We're going to sit you up there
on that front row up there.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:04.420
This year's winner is a
fulfillment of a prediction

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:06.550
I made--

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:10.360
the prediction I made when we
hired a certain faculty member,

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:13.840
and I said, if we heard that
guy, the first thing that's

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:16.760
going to happen is the
classes are going to swell up

00:29:16.760 --> 00:29:18.910
and we're going
to have hire more.

00:29:18.910 --> 00:29:21.790
This is the more.

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:23.690
He's a fine young man.

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:26.210
He's a superb scholar.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:28.090
He's an excellent
colleague to the people

00:29:28.090 --> 00:29:29.530
in the German department.

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:31.810
Yaniga was the one we hired.

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:33.526
And I could just see--

00:29:33.526 --> 00:29:36.680
the great Eberhard Geyer was
the chairman of that department

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:42.030
then, and it's just grown.

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:45.860
Stephan Naumann has
been here since 2013.

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:50.602
He got a BA in pre-seminary
studies in 2001.

00:29:50.602 --> 00:29:51.560
Oh, I didn't know this.

00:29:54.070 --> 00:29:55.580
You should all study with him.

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:58.570
He has a bachelor's degree
in tourism and travel,

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:00.070
as well as German.

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:01.615
Get that guy to
take you to Germany.

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:08.080
MA and PhD in German studies.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:12.760
He spent time in Germany and
Poland, where he met his wife.

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:16.740
His German and
Polish are fluent.

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:20.260
He knows a little
Spanish, he says.

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:22.390
He learned-- see, that's
the way I put it--

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:24.340
he learned ancient Greek--

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:26.320
also biblical Hebrew and Latin--

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:27.790
while at pre-seminary.

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:29.770
I like to say that,
the past tense.

00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:31.780
I learned Greek.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:33.190
Do I remember any?

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:33.750
Not enough.

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:43.850
Stephen Naumann is a
blessing to the college,

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:45.600
and we are grateful to have him.

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:48.416
Please come forward
with Fred Yaniga.

00:30:48.416 --> 00:30:55.886
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:37.900
Sorry.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:39.400
Hey, guys, I forgot
about the money.

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:48.030
Naumann gets money and
the department gets money.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:50.895
And I think that's
Blackstock's idea,

00:31:50.895 --> 00:31:52.145
so that they wouldn't quarrel.

00:31:59.845 --> 00:32:05.737
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:17.300
Was anybody else
particularly struck

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:20.240
by the grade point
averages this year?

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:23.690
Do you realize that the
academic standards of the girls

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:27.960
and the boys at Hillsdale
College are the same?

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:31.130
Did you realize that we
have as many applicants--

00:32:31.130 --> 00:32:33.830
or now a few more lately--
from boys than from girls?

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:36.120
Nobody gets that.

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:39.110
And yet the boys, what are you--

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:42.290
because you're not dumb.

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:45.710
It's got to be bad character.

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:46.520
Straighten up.

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:52.820
I'm not going to comment
on whether the scholarship

00:32:52.820 --> 00:32:57.511
winner among the fraternities
had a higher or lower grade

00:32:57.511 --> 00:33:00.800
point average the
men's average--

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:04.950
which is, of course, notoriously
lower than the women's average.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:07.940
So if we compared
those numbers, we

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:12.290
might think the Greek
boys ought to study more.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:13.090
I'm just saying.

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:19.340
You're all doing
fine, and we probably

00:33:19.340 --> 00:33:21.110
won't throw you out of here--

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:21.610
probably.

00:33:35.750 --> 00:33:38.990
We don't pay Michael
Ward enough money for us

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:41.900
to call him our
own, but if somebody

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:44.960
will please work out a deal
with him, then we will.

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:49.650
I'm tired of hinting about it.

00:33:54.350 --> 00:33:57.350
He is a remarkable man.

00:33:57.350 --> 00:34:01.070
He's got long experience as
a chaplain and a professor

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:03.870
in both Oxford and Cambridge.

00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:07.159
He's a fellow at
Houston Baptist.

00:34:07.159 --> 00:34:08.659
And I don't know
what he does there,

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:11.540
but whatever that is,
he should do it here.

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:21.550
There are two distinctive
things about him.

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:24.400
One is I've called him before
the best student of CS Lewis,

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:25.330
and he is that.

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:28.000
I think that more
now than I did then.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:31.570
His book Planet
Narnia is tremendous.

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:36.679
And what he did was just take
seriously what the book meant,

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:42.030
which is what professors
do, when they are any good.

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:44.460
And it's a revelation
to read it.

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:46.270
And it makes you
marvel at a thing

00:34:46.270 --> 00:34:49.480
that you may already
have loved, because you

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:52.840
see that it is a work
of art on a higher level

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:53.605
than you realized.

00:34:53.605 --> 00:34:54.730
That's what happened to me.

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:02.850
The second thing is he's
got this wicked wit.

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:06.060
He's been here twice before to
give important speeches-- once

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:08.970
commencement.

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:10.890
And in this
commencement speech, he

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:13.470
gave one of the finest
commencement speeches any of us

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:15.020
has ever heard.

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:17.102
But it began with
about five minutes

00:35:17.102 --> 00:35:18.810
of making fun of the
name of the college.

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:24.870
Because it is a
weird name, isn't it?

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.960
You ever heard anything
else named Hillsdale?

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:29.370
There's only one
place I know of,

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:33.500
and it was directly copied
after us, down in Oklahoma.

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:36.540
And why?

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.160
Are you going to do it again?

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:39.500
I'm going to remind him.

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:44.780
He said, is it a dale
belonging to a hill?

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:45.890
It's kind of silly.

00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:48.950
And then he redeemed himself
by associating the name

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:52.990
with an imaginary world
he made up as a kid called

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:54.540
Tor or something--

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:56.450
Tordale, right.

00:35:56.450 --> 00:35:59.650
It's a synonym for Hillsdale.

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:01.340
By the way, that's
the kind of thing

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:04.228
Lewis and Tolkien did
when they were kids,

00:36:04.228 --> 00:36:05.270
and when they were grown.

00:36:05.270 --> 00:36:08.580
They made up the fantasy world
and they named stuff in them.

00:36:08.580 --> 00:36:11.360
So this is another
third really weird guy.

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:20.310
We asked him to speak at the
groundbreaking of this chapel.

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:23.020
And that was just
right to do, because

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:25.540
of what he is, what he
represents in the world,

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:28.930
so much like what
we try to represent.

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:32.140
And now we've asked him to
speak at our first convocation--

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:35.080
also extremely appropriate--

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.644
Michael Ward.

00:36:36.644 --> 00:36:39.129
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:55.170
MICHAEL WARD: Good
morning, everybody.

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:56.420
Thank you for having me back.

00:36:56.420 --> 00:37:00.130
Yes, I was at the groundbreaking
2 and 1/2 years ago,

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:04.010
and it's a huge privilege now
to see this building complete

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:05.240
and fully splendid.

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:08.360
I congratulate you,
President Arnn, and indeed,

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:11.030
everybody concerned--
builders, architects,

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:12.470
college in general--

00:37:12.470 --> 00:37:17.470
in bringing this massive project
to a successful conclusion.

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:19.900
Last time I was here,
I seem to recall,

00:37:19.900 --> 00:37:24.070
I attempted a little
spirited wordplay--

00:37:24.070 --> 00:37:26.830
not about Hillsdale on
my most recent visit,

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:31.270
but on Dr. Arnn's academic
expertise in Churchill,

00:37:31.270 --> 00:37:33.940
and on this church on a hill.

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:39.970
And here it is.

00:37:39.970 --> 00:37:43.840
But of course, what we
find ourselves within today

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:45.970
is not strictly a church.

00:37:45.970 --> 00:37:48.060
It is a chapel.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:49.690
And so I want to
begin my remarks

00:37:49.690 --> 00:37:55.670
by asking why this building
has been called Christ Chapel.

00:37:55.670 --> 00:37:58.680
Well, there are several
answers to that question.

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:00.920
It's been called
Christ Chapel partly

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:04.250
to reflect the devout
Christian faith

00:38:04.250 --> 00:38:08.270
of the principal benefactors,
Jack and Jo Babbitt.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:12.290
It's partly Christ Chapel
because its architecture gives

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:14.900
homage to that church
in Philadelphia,

00:38:14.900 --> 00:38:17.180
where America's Founding
Fathers worshipped

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:19.380
during the Continental Congress.

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:22.470
And I'll be coming back to
that church in Philly later on.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:25.940
For now, I just want to
note that that church was

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:29.770
and is called Christ Church.

00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:31.600
This is Christ Chapel.

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:34.840
So what is the distinguishing
feature of a chapel?

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:38.810
What is the etymology
of the word chapel?

00:38:38.810 --> 00:38:40.530
And to answer those
questions, I invite

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:44.050
you to accompany me in
imagination back in time

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:47.120
to fourth century Europe.

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:50.870
It's the early fourth century,
and the emperor Constantine

00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:54.410
is presiding over
the Roman Empire.

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:58.550
We find ourselves in a part
of Europe somewhat to the east

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:01.490
and somewhat to the
south, in what would today

00:39:01.490 --> 00:39:03.610
be called Hungary.

00:39:03.610 --> 00:39:06.830
We're in a province
called Pannonia,

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:09.920
and we enter the house of a
soldier, whose wife has just

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:12.543
given birth to a baby boy.

00:39:12.543 --> 00:39:14.210
The mother and the
father give their son

00:39:14.210 --> 00:39:19.370
the name Martin, from Martinus,
diminutive of Martius,

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:21.950
pertaining to Mars--

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:25.220
a highly appropriate name, not
only because the boy's father

00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:28.550
is in the Roman army, but
because Martin himself

00:39:28.550 --> 00:39:33.800
will soon grow up and enter
upon a military career himself.

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:36.770
He becomes an officer,
and is assigned to duty

00:39:36.770 --> 00:39:40.490
in Gaul, modern-day France.

00:39:40.490 --> 00:39:43.490
On garrison duty at
Amiens one winter's

00:39:43.490 --> 00:39:47.930
day, as he rides through
the city gates on his horse,

00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:52.100
Martin does something that is
so unexpected, so memorable, so

00:39:52.100 --> 00:39:55.310
striking that it's been
recounted in literature

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:58.640
and represented
in art ever since.

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:01.640
On that cold day,
this young tribune,

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:04.490
dressed in the
regalia of his unit--

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:09.380
gleaming armor, ridged
helmet, large white cloak

00:40:09.380 --> 00:40:11.720
lined with lamb's wool--

00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:15.050
he sees a poor man
begging by the roadside--

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:18.080
a man whose clothes are
so ragged that he's hardly

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:20.720
covered at all.

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:23.430
The beggar is shaking
with the cold,

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:27.890
and Martin, on an impulse,
jumps from his horse,

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:31.580
commands two of his retinue
to dismount with him,

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:35.270
takes off his large white
cloak, orders his two men

00:40:35.270 --> 00:40:38.360
to hold it out taut between
them, and then with one

00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:43.660
quick stroke of his sword, he
slashes the garment in two.

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:46.150
He then hands one
half to the beggar,

00:40:46.150 --> 00:40:49.060
pulls the remaining half around
his own shoulders, remounts,

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:51.490
and rides on his way.

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:54.100
That night, he dreams.

00:40:54.100 --> 00:41:00.070
He dreams and sees Christ in
heaven saying to the angels,

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:04.540
Martin clothed me with his robe.

00:41:04.540 --> 00:41:06.570
And after Martin's
death many years later--

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:08.720
he died in the year 397--

00:41:08.720 --> 00:41:11.240
the part of the
cloak that he kept

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:14.480
was preserved in his
honor in an oratory

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:18.770
of the Abbey at Tours in the
middle of France, 150 miles

00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:21.830
southwest of Paris.

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:25.520
And what does all this have
to do with the word "chapel"?

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:27.890
Let us consult the
dictionary, which tells us

00:41:27.890 --> 00:41:32.450
that the word "chapel"
derives from the capela,

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:36.650
or cloak, of St. Martin,
preserved by the Frankish kings

00:41:36.650 --> 00:41:38.420
as a sacred relic.

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:40.220
The term chapel,
the dictionary says,

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:42.470
was applied to the
sanctuary in which

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:46.310
this cloak was preserved under
the care of its capellani--

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:49.280
or chaplains-- and
thence, generally,

00:41:49.280 --> 00:41:52.670
to a sanctuary containing
holy relics, and finally

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:55.640
to any building for
orisons or worship

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:58.580
not being a regular church.

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:01.160
Thus says the Oxford
English Dictionary,

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:03.440
and if Oxford says
so, it must be true.

00:42:06.480 --> 00:42:10.580
So this soldier, this
caped crusader, Martin,

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:14.990
is now known to the world
as St. Martin of Tours--

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:19.870
T-O-U-R-S. The S is silent.

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:25.940
But if you are Churchillian,
you would say "tours."

00:42:25.940 --> 00:42:28.250
Churchill scorned
those English travelers

00:42:28.250 --> 00:42:31.910
who insisted on saying they'd
visited Marseilles, Lyons,

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:33.350
and Paris.

00:42:33.350 --> 00:42:36.260
No, he said, you visited
"Mar-sails," "Lions,"

00:42:36.260 --> 00:42:37.040
and "Pair-us."

00:42:40.510 --> 00:42:43.460
Foreign names were made for
Englishmen, not Englishmen

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:46.470
for foreign names.

00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:49.880
And I'm sure Churchill would
have called this Roman soldier

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:53.060
St. Martin of "Tours"--

00:42:53.060 --> 00:42:56.990
patron saint of
package holidays.

00:42:56.990 --> 00:43:00.000
In fact, St. Martin is
the patron saint of--

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.460
among other things-- the
US Army's Quartermaster d

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:07.820
And so popular was Martin for
many centuries after his death

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:12.620
that Martin Luther, the great
reformer, was named after him,

00:43:12.620 --> 00:43:17.090
and baptized on his feast
day, November the 11th.

00:43:17.090 --> 00:43:20.390
We marked it in the church
calendar on Monday this week.

00:43:20.390 --> 00:43:21.920
And this whole
season of the year

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:25.778
is known in some
places as Martinmas.

00:43:25.778 --> 00:43:26.570
You have Christmas.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:27.362
You have Candlemas.

00:43:27.362 --> 00:43:28.380
You have Michaelmas.

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:30.420
You also have Martinmas.

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:33.990
The University of St. Andrews
in Scotland, where I got my PhD,

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:39.190
calls this semester Martinmas
in their academic calendar.

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:43.750
There's an old proverb that
runs, "his Martinmas must come,

00:43:43.750 --> 00:43:46.750
as it does to every hog."

00:43:46.750 --> 00:43:50.860
In other words, all must
die, because November

00:43:50.860 --> 00:43:54.040
was the great slaughtering
time of the Anglo-Saxons,

00:43:54.040 --> 00:43:57.490
when fodder was all used up,
and oxen, sheep, and hogs

00:43:57.490 --> 00:43:59.590
were killed and
salted for the winter.

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:05.360
So we've already found two
links between ourselves

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:09.230
here today in Christ
Chapel and Martin of Tours.

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:13.520
This building is called a chapel
because of his bisected capela,

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:15.830
and this time of year
is Martinmas because

00:44:15.830 --> 00:44:18.700
of his November feast today.

00:44:18.700 --> 00:44:22.450
Can we find a third
link with St. Martin?

00:44:22.450 --> 00:44:24.790
Pleasingly, we can.

00:44:24.790 --> 00:44:28.090
This third link is via that
building in Philadelphia

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:33.370
that I mentioned earlier,
for Christ Church in Philly--

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:36.460
on which this
building is modeled--

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:39.970
was itself modeled on a famous
church in central London

00:44:39.970 --> 00:44:43.960
called St. Martin in the Fields.

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:46.180
As the name in the
fields would imply,

00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:49.210
that church originally stood
out in the countryside.

00:44:49.210 --> 00:44:51.190
But the city gradually
surrounded it,

00:44:51.190 --> 00:44:54.100
and St. Martin in
the Fields is now

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:56.770
right in the heart of
the British capital,

00:44:56.770 --> 00:45:00.040
next to Trafalgar Square
and the National Gallery.

00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:03.370
It's one of the best-known
churches in England.

00:45:03.370 --> 00:45:05.800
And the interior
of this building

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:08.830
owes an unmistakable
debt to that church--

00:45:08.830 --> 00:45:11.527
the Corinthian columns,
the barrel ceiling,

00:45:11.527 --> 00:45:12.610
the shape of the windows--

00:45:12.610 --> 00:45:16.640
especially the tripartite
window behind me.

00:45:16.640 --> 00:45:20.900
Christ Chapel Hillsdale is
the architectural grandchild

00:45:20.900 --> 00:45:24.730
of St. Martin in the Fields.

00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:27.340
So we have a threefold
link to Martin of Tours--

00:45:27.340 --> 00:45:29.830
the etymological link,
the calendrical link,

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:31.990
the architectural link.

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:34.660
And a scripture reminds
us, a threefold cord

00:45:34.660 --> 00:45:37.600
is not easily broken.

00:45:37.600 --> 00:45:40.060
So let's think more
deeply about Martin

00:45:40.060 --> 00:45:42.550
and his startling act
of generosity that

00:45:42.550 --> 00:45:46.180
has never been forgotten.

00:45:46.180 --> 00:45:48.620
How foolish of
him, we might say,

00:45:48.620 --> 00:45:51.250
if we were in a skeptical
or a cynical mood.

00:45:51.250 --> 00:45:53.410
How foolish of him
to start chopping up

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:56.080
his own clothes like that.

00:45:56.080 --> 00:45:57.870
Wouldn't it have made
much better sense

00:45:57.870 --> 00:46:00.810
to give the beggar money with
which he could have bought

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:04.890
himself a complete new cloak?

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:07.050
How impulsive and
how impractical.

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:10.530
How non-universalizable.

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:13.890
For after all, what about the
next beggar that he meets?

00:46:13.890 --> 00:46:16.930
Will that beggar get
a quarter of the cloak

00:46:16.930 --> 00:46:19.020
and the next beggar an eighth?

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:21.210
Where will it all end?

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:24.330
Jesus says in the gospels,
whoever has two cloaks,

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:26.880
let him share with
him who has none.

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:29.580
He said nothing about
slicing your cloaks in half.

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:35.590
Well, these sorts of questions
betray a rather pedantic

00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:38.920
mindset, and they
overlook a crucial dream

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:41.860
that gives us the context
for the whole interaction.

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:44.890
In the dream,
Christ says, Martin

00:46:44.890 --> 00:46:47.610
clothed me with his robe.

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:51.700
So the beggar turns out to be
a rather important character

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:54.460
in this memorable
incident unfolding

00:46:54.460 --> 00:46:57.070
by the gates of Amiens--

00:46:57.070 --> 00:47:00.680
or as Churchill would
say, "Am-eye-ens."

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:03.050
It would seem it's
not so much Martin,

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:06.530
but the shivering wretch by
the side of the road who's

00:47:06.530 --> 00:47:09.670
the holy hero of the story.

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:13.890
Well, then, should we
imitate the beggar?

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:15.630
Should we dress
ourselves in rags,

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:18.105
give away all our
advantages, move to France?

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:22.975
England maybe, France never.

00:47:22.975 --> 00:47:26.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:30.820
But again, these questions
betray a small-minded focus

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:33.490
on dragging some
policy proposal out

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:36.250
of this memorable encounter.

00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:39.010
Martin and the beggar
met in the Roman Empire

00:47:39.010 --> 00:47:41.680
of the fourth century,
where interaction is not

00:47:41.680 --> 00:47:46.240
a template for social services
or the charitable sector

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:49.270
in a modern Western democracy.

00:47:49.270 --> 00:47:51.940
Rather, I think,
it's a perennially

00:47:51.940 --> 00:48:00.350
telling moral and spiritual
story about a sword and cloak.

00:48:00.350 --> 00:48:04.900
Let's approach this story
poetically, symbolically.

00:48:04.900 --> 00:48:07.330
Martin bore a sword.

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:08.650
He had power.

00:48:08.650 --> 00:48:09.940
He had strength.

00:48:09.940 --> 00:48:11.480
He wielded military might.

00:48:11.480 --> 00:48:13.330
He enforced the Pax Romana.

00:48:13.330 --> 00:48:16.150
No doubt he was a strong man
physically, who could actually

00:48:16.150 --> 00:48:18.520
do harm with that weapon.

00:48:18.520 --> 00:48:21.660
He had a sword.

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:24.300
But he also had a cloak.

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:27.990
Let us picture it as a warm,
soft, thick, richly-embroidered

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:30.720
garment, so very different
from the tattered rags

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:32.740
of the beggar.

00:48:32.740 --> 00:48:36.570
The poor man's need was
on display for all to see,

00:48:36.570 --> 00:48:41.160
but Martin, underneath
his garments,

00:48:41.160 --> 00:48:45.030
was just another man like him.

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:48.080
What was the difference
between the two?

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:50.450
I'm put in mind of
Shakespeare's King Lear, who

00:48:50.450 --> 00:48:52.940
tells us, "Through
tattered clothes,

00:48:52.940 --> 00:48:55.220
great devices do appear.

00:48:55.220 --> 00:49:00.480
Robes and furred
gowns hide all."

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:02.460
Clothing masks.

00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:05.010
Clothing hides.

00:49:05.010 --> 00:49:08.880
Without clothing, we're ashamed
and naked, like Adam and Eve

00:49:08.880 --> 00:49:10.510
after the fall.

00:49:10.510 --> 00:49:13.280
Everyone must have clothes.

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:18.590
They're a symbol of our
human frailty, of our need.

00:49:18.590 --> 00:49:21.740
And perhaps more, especially
for those who pride themselves

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:24.620
on their power,
clothes are needed

00:49:24.620 --> 00:49:30.110
to preserve that fragile
pretense of invulnerability.

00:49:30.110 --> 00:49:33.800
For which of us
is never in need?

00:49:33.800 --> 00:49:38.660
We here today don't lack
clothes on our backs, thank God.

00:49:38.660 --> 00:49:41.270
But do we lack nothing?

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:43.580
How many of us are
privately struggling

00:49:43.580 --> 00:49:46.740
with all sorts of
difficulties and deficiencies

00:49:46.740 --> 00:49:49.260
this very morning?

00:49:49.260 --> 00:49:51.660
Our relationships
are going awry.

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:54.840
Our health is giving
us pause for thought.

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:57.060
We're worried about our exams.

00:49:57.060 --> 00:50:00.650
Where are we going
in life, and why?

00:50:00.650 --> 00:50:03.610
But even if we're wholly
secure in our relationships,

00:50:03.610 --> 00:50:06.280
even if we're utterly
confident about our abilities,

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:09.970
even if we've won first prize in
all the competitions we've ever

00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:15.610
entered or ever
will enter, so what?

00:50:15.610 --> 00:50:19.570
Our Martinmas must come,
as it does to every hog.

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:24.630
So this is where
we would do well,

00:50:24.630 --> 00:50:29.100
I think, to consider the
sword and the cloak together,

00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:30.690
where they meet.

00:50:30.690 --> 00:50:36.240
Think of that sharp sword
and that stylish cloak.

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:40.840
And now, see the one
slicing the other in half.

00:50:40.840 --> 00:50:47.220
See the frayed edge where
the sword went through.

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:52.560
But the sword went throughout
of generosity, nothing worse--

00:50:52.560 --> 00:50:55.110
out of largeness of heart.

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:58.470
Martin's act may
have been impulsive,

00:50:58.470 --> 00:51:02.820
but the impulse was one of
magnanimity, not one of hatred,

00:51:02.820 --> 00:51:05.660
or despair, or violence.

00:51:05.660 --> 00:51:11.210
His act was a recognition
that all human beings have

00:51:11.210 --> 00:51:14.750
to shiver when they're cold.

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:17.990
So let us at least
stand by one another

00:51:17.990 --> 00:51:23.430
compassionately, now giving,
now receiving, now feeling

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:25.650
our power, now
knowing our weakness.

00:51:28.310 --> 00:51:30.560
It's curious when you think
about it really, isn't it,

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:37.020
that a chapel should be a place
for a torn cape, a fragment

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:39.630
of cloth.

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:44.280
How would it be, though, if
the cloak was still intact,

00:51:44.280 --> 00:51:49.212
testimony only to Martin's
talent for self-preservation?

00:51:49.212 --> 00:51:50.670
That would not have
been worked out

00:51:50.670 --> 00:51:53.880
housing in a place of
honor, for there's nothing

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:57.890
very special about looking
out for number one.

00:51:57.890 --> 00:52:03.660
But a cloak rent asunder, that's
another thing altogether--

00:52:03.660 --> 00:52:05.940
bisected for all
the right reasons.

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:08.830
That's a cape of good hope.

00:52:08.830 --> 00:52:12.230
It points us to other
severances, doesn't it?

00:52:12.230 --> 00:52:15.650
More profound, eternal
severances even--

00:52:15.650 --> 00:52:18.500
think of the curtain of
the temple torn in two.

00:52:18.500 --> 00:52:22.610
Think of Christ's side
pierced with a spear.

00:52:22.610 --> 00:52:26.090
Think of the rock in the desert
gushing forth water, when

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:29.060
Moses struck it with a rod.

00:52:29.060 --> 00:52:33.630
The gash admits the grace.

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:37.520
It's the crack where
the light gets in,

00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:40.350
as Leonard Cohen almost said.

00:52:40.350 --> 00:52:44.970
And we all have swords and
cloaks of various kinds.

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:46.750
We have powers.

00:52:46.750 --> 00:52:48.390
We have needs.

00:52:48.390 --> 00:52:50.100
We have abilities.

00:52:50.100 --> 00:52:53.060
We have deficiencies.

00:52:53.060 --> 00:52:57.350
So this Martinmas, in this
marvelous, martial chapel,

00:52:57.350 --> 00:53:01.220
I propose that we both
celebrate the sword

00:53:01.220 --> 00:53:04.620
and acknowledge the cloak--

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:09.060
and above all, hallow the
intersection of the two,

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:14.110
where, in weakness,
strength is made perfect.

00:53:17.973 --> 00:53:18.971
Thank you.

00:53:18.971 --> 00:53:24.959
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:53.706 --> 00:53:55.748
REV. ADAM RICK: Please
stand for the benediction.

00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:03.510
And now, by the grace
of almighty God,

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:05.310
may you be filled
with the knowledge

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:09.010
of his will and all
wisdom and understanding,

00:54:09.010 --> 00:54:12.600
so as to walk in a manner
worthy of the Lord,

00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:14.310
fully pleasing to
Him, and bearing

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:17.080
fruit in every good work.

00:54:17.080 --> 00:54:19.510
And may you be
strengthened with all power

00:54:19.510 --> 00:54:24.550
according to His glorious might,
for all endurance and patience

00:54:24.550 --> 00:54:28.740
with joy, giving thanks
to Him in all things.

00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:31.660
And the blessing of God
Almighty, the Father, the Son,

00:54:31.660 --> 00:54:35.510
and the Holy Spirit be amongst
you and remain with you always.

00:54:35.510 --> 00:54:36.010
Amen.

00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:44.160
[ORGAN PLAYING]

